'''''Euphorbia serrata''''' is a species of [[spurge]] known by the common names '''serrated spurge''' and '''sawtooth spurge''', and also known as '''Tintern spurge''' and '''upright spurge'''.<ref>{{cite web |title=Euphorbia serrata {{!}} Tintern spurge /RHS Gardening |url=https://www.rhs.org.uk/plants/19855/upright-spurge/details |website=www.rhs.org.uk |access-date=10 March 2024 |language=en-gb}}</ref> It is native to Europe but it is present elsewhere as a weedy [[introduced species]].<ref name=jep>{{cite web |title=Archived TJM 1993 treatment for EUPHORBIA serrata |url=http://ucjeps.berkeley.edu/cgi-bin/get_JM_treatment.pl?3618,3662,3675 |website=ucjeps.berkeley.edu |access-date=10 March 2024}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=USDA Plants Database |url=https://plants.usda.gov/java/profile?symbol=EUSE12 |website=plants.usda.gov |access-date=10 March 2024}}</ref> This is a perennial herb growing anywhere from 20 centimetres to about half a metre in height.<ref name=jep/> The leaves are long and very narrow on most of the plant, with more oval-shaped leaves toward the tips of the stems.<ref name=jep/><ref>{{cite web |title=CalPhotos |url=http://calphotos.berkeley.edu/cgi/img_query?query_src=photos_index&where-taxon=Euphorbia+serrata |website=calphotos.berkeley.edu |access-date=10 March 2024}}</ref> They are finely toothed.<ref name=jep/> At the ends of the branches are [[inflorescence]]s of tiny flowers.{{citation needed|date=December 2020}} The fruit is a spherical capsule about half a centimetre wide containing tiny gray seeds.<ref name=jep/>

